$(BUGSTITLE_TEXT_BODY Compiler changes,

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.bitfield-default-init,Support for default values in C-style bitfields)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.bitfield-default-init.dd)
$(P
D now supports specifying default values for C-style bitfields in structs and classes.
This brings valid D code closer to behavior allowed in C++20 and allows for more concise initialization of bitfield members.
)

$(P
Value range checking is performed at compile-time to ensure the default value fits within the specified number of bits.
)

-------
struct S
{
    int a : 4 = 2;   // OK
    int b : 2 = 5;   // Error: bitfield initializer `5` does not fit in 2 bits
    uint c : 1 = 1;  // OK
    bool d : 1 = 1;  // OK
}
-------
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.exception-rewrite,Destructors now run in a `nothrow` function when an `Error` unwinds through it)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.exception-rewrite.dd)
$(P
Destructors and `scope(exit)` blocks internally generate try-finally statements.
Since 2018, DMD has optimized `finally` blocks in `nothrow` try bodies by rewriting them into a simple sequence,
avoiding the overhead of exception unwinding. However, this optimization also skipped `finally` blocks when
an `Error` was thrown, preventing expected cleanup from running.
)

$(P
This optimization has been reverted to the pre-2018 behavior: `finally` blocks always run, regardless of whether
the try body can throw an `Exception`.
)

$(P
To reenable the optimization, use the new `-nothrow-optimizations` switch.
)

$(P
The following example demonstrates the fix - "exiting" is now printed even though `callMe` is a `nothrow` function:
)

```d
import core.stdc.stdio;

void main()
{
    S s2;
    callMe();
}

struct S
{
    ~this()
    {
        printf("exiting\n");
    }
}

void callMe() nothrow
{
    throw new Error("hi there :)");
}
```

$(P
The `-betterC` switch is unaffected by this change.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.fastdfa,New experimental Data Flow Analysis Engine for nullability and truthiness)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.fastdfa.dd)
$(P
A new experimental Data Flow Analysis (DFA) has been implemented under the preview flag ``-preview=fastdfa``.
The intent of the engine is to be both fast and free from false positives; if successful, it may in the future be turned on by default.
)

$(P
No attributes have been implemented to date; before they are considered, the engine itself must be both usable with the right tradeoffs and have desirable features.
This has some side effects: it prevents separate compilation, function pointers, and cyclic functions from being analysable.
These limitations are not supposed to prevent a successful compilation when in use.
)

$(P
The engine itself is variable centric with a strong focus on giving up on analysing a variable if things get too complex for it.
This can result in messages that may not appear to make sense for where they are emitted, due to the way shortcutting of analysis works.
As an example of loops:
)

```d
void loopy()
{
    int* ptr = new int;

    foreach (i; 0 .. 2) // Error: Variable `ptr` was required to be non-null and has become null
    {
        int val = *ptr;
        ptr = null;
    }
}
```

$(P
If the engine is successful, the reporting mechanism would be replaced with a tracing state pass.
This would offer a line by line explanation of how and why the engine thought something was true for a function.
)

$(P
The engine has been tested on a 100k LOC defensively written codebase without any false positives.
The performance is similar to DIP1000 and is not supposed to be noticeable.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.fastdfa.uninitialized,Fast DFA reports on uninitialized variable reads)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.fastdfa.uninitialized.dd)
$(P
The fast DFA engine has gained the ability to error when a variable can be proven to be in an uninitialized state, and is read.
)

```d
void readFromUninit() @system
{
    int val1 = void;
    int val2 = val1; // error

    int* ptr = &val1;
    int val3 = *ptr; // error
}
```

$(P
As part of this it is now capable of looking through pointers and seeing properties of variables that are on the stack.
)

$(P
An application of this feature is the prevention of mathematical operators from accessing default initialized floating point types.
)

```d
void checkFloatInit(bool condition)
{
    float v;
    float t = v * 2; // error math op

    if (condition)
        v = 2;

    float u = v * 2; // no error
}
```

$(P
It will not activate for other expressions, or statements.
Only in a mathematical expression.
)

$(P
This was not originally part of the fast DFA engine's scope; its continued existence depends upon community feedback as part of usage.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.ftime-trace-template-args,Improve `-ftime-trace` template instance detail)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.ftime-trace-template-args.dd)
$(P
The `-ftime-trace` profiling output now includes template argument types
in the event name for template instance events, allowing users to
distinguish between different instantiations of the same template
(e.g. `isArray!(int)` instead of just `isArray`).
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.needsDestruction,Added `__traits(needsDestruction, T)`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.needsDestruction.dd)
$(P
True if T is a *value* type needing elaborate destruction. This includes structs with explicit `~this()` destructors and/or compiler-generated destructors (caused by fields needing destruction), static arrays thereof, and enums with such base types.
)

---
class C { ~this(); }
struct S { ~this(); }

static assert(!__traits(needsDestruction, C));
static assert(__traits(needsDestruction, S));
static assert(!__traits(needsDestruction, S[0]));
static assert(__traits(needsDestruction, S[1]));
---
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.isOverlapped,New trait `__traits(isOverlapped, field)` to detect overlapping fields)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.isOverlapped.dd)
$(P
D now provides a compile-time trait to check whether a struct or class field overlaps with other fields in memory. This is useful for serialization libraries, code generators, and metaprogramming tasks that need to identify fields sharing the same memory location.
)

$(P
The trait takes a single field argument, returning `true` if the field's storage overlaps with other fields (typically because it is part of a union).
)

```d
struct S
{
    int a;
    union
    {
        int x;  // overlaps with y
        float y;  // overlaps with x
    }
    int b;
}

static assert(__traits(isOverlapped, S.x));  // true
static assert(__traits(isOverlapped, S.y));  // true
static assert(!__traits(isOverlapped, S.a)); // false - regular field
static assert(!__traits(isOverlapped, S.b)); // false - regular field
```

$(P
The trait works with both anonymous and named unions:
)

```d
union NamedUnion
{
    int x;
    float y;
}

static assert(__traits(isOverlapped, NamedUnion.x));  // true
static assert(__traits(isOverlapped, NamedUnion.y));  // true
```

$(P
This trait is particularly useful for:
- Serialization libraries that need to handle only one field from overlapping sets
- Understanding memory layout and field interaction
- Implementing correct destructors for types with overlapping fields
- Generic code that needs to reason about field storage semantics
)

$(P
The trait exposes DMD's internal overlap tracking (`VarDeclaration.overlapped`), providing a direct way to query this semantic property.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.nullderefcheck,Checks for null dereferences)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.nullderefcheck.dd)
$(P
A new check has been implemented that injects code to check a pointer for null before it is dereferenced.
)

$(P
It will typically be used if you need a backtrace generated or you want to catch and handle the `Error` as part of a scheduler.
)

$(P
This can be enabled for all functions by using `-check=nullderef=on` or exclusively for `@safe` functions with `-check=nullderef=safeonly`.
By default it is off.
What happens may be customized by the `-checkaction` switch and by setting a new handler in `core.exception`.
)

---
@safe void foo()
{
    int* p;
    // if -check=nullderef=on or -check=nullderef=safeonly is set:
    int x = *p; // null dereference error thrown at runtime
}

@system void bar()
{
    int* p;
    // if -check=nullderef=on is set:
    int x = *p; // no explicit check; relies on OS signal/exception
}
---

$(P
Due to issues in dmd's backend, not all pointer dereferences are guaranteed to get a check.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.saferPrintf,Allow certain `pragma(printf)` function calls to be treated as `@safe`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.saferPrintf.dd)
$(P
`printf` function calls in general are unsafe. Many calls, however,
can be automatically checked for safety.
)

$(P
This change allows calling a $(DDSUBLINK spec/pragma, printf,
`pragma(printf)`) function from @safe code when:
)

$(P
- the callee is marked `@safe` or `@trusted`
- the format string passed is a literal
- no string format specifiers are used
)

$(P
Note: `pragma(printf)` already enforces type safety.
)

$(P
For example:
)

```d
extern(C) pragma(printf)
void printf(const char* format, ...) @trusted;

@safe void func(int i, char* s)
{
    printf("i is %d\n", i);  // allowed
    printf("s is %s\n", s);  // Error: call is not safe
    printf(s);               // Error: call is not safe
}
```
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.sarr-length-infer,Add support for static array length inference)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.sarr-length-infer.dd)
$(P
Added support for using `$` to automatically infer the length of a static array from its initializer. This allows for cleaner declarations without manually counting elements.
)

```d
int[$] arr = [1, 2, 3]; // Length is inferred as 3
```
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.two-pass-inliner,The compiler now inlines `pragma(inline, true)` functions in a separate pass)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.two-pass-inliner.dd)
$(P
The compiler now considers `pragma(inline, true)` functions for inlining in a dedicated pass
before trying to inline other eligible functions. This provides better control of inlining
decisions, for example:
)

```d
auto staticSquares(uint n)()
{
    int[n] arr;
    static foreach(i; 0 .. n)
        arr[i] = (i + 1) ^^ 2;
    return arr;
}

pragma(inline, true) int thirtyThirty()
{
    return staticSquares!30[$ - 1];
}
```

$(P
Previously, `dmd -inline` would first inline `staticSquares()` into `thirtyThirty()` and
subsequently fail to inline `thirtyThirty()` into its callers. With the new implementation,
the programmer can expect the compiler to replace calls to `thirtyThirty()` with
`staticSquares!30[$ - 1]` before making other inlining decisions.
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.vgc-closure,`-vgc` now reports locations of nested functions that create closures)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.vgc-closure.dd)
$(P
When a GC-allocated closure was created, the `-vgc` switch would only point to the outer function that a closure was created for.
For a big function, this still requires you to search where the nested function requiring the closure actually is.
It now reports the closure function and variable location just like in error messages for `@nogc`.
)

```
auto foo()
{
    int x;
    int bar() { return x; }
    return &bar;
}
```

$(P
Before:
)

$(P
$(CONSOLE
vgc.d(1): vgc: using closure causes GC allocation
)
)

$(P
After:
)

$(P
$(CONSOLE
vgc.d(1): vgc: using closure causes GC allocation
vgc.d(4): vgc: function `bar` closes over variable `x`
vgc.d(3): vgc: `x` declared here
)
)
)

$(LI $(LNAME2 dmd.with-assign,Add support for `with (auto x = expression())`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/dmd.with-assign.dd)
$(P
Added support for using `with` statements with an expression initialiser as an `AssignExpression`, like `if`, `while`, `for` and `switch`.
For backwards compatibility, `with` still also accepts a qualified expression without assignment to a variable, e.g. `with (immutable expression())`.
)
)


)

$(BUGSTITLE_TEXT_BODY Runtime changes,

$(LI $(LNAME2 druntime.filterthrown,Gravedigger approach to `Throwable`s escaping a thread entry point is now available)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dmd, changelog/druntime.filterthrown.dd)
$(P
A new method is added to `ThreadBase` enabling filtering of any `Throwable` before it is handled by the thread abstraction.
This may be used per-thread and globally, to log that an `Error` has occurred or to exit the process.
)

$(P
A reasonable error handler that may be of use is:
)

```d
import core.exception;

void main()
{
    filterThreadThrowableHandler = (ref Throwable t) {
        import core.stdc.stdio;
        import core.stdc.stdlib;

        if (auto e = cast(Error) t)
        {
            auto msg = e.message();
            fprintf(stderr, "Thread death due to error: %.*s\n", cast(int)msg.length, msg.ptr);
            fflush(stderr);
            abort();
        }
    };
}
```

$(P
For a per thread handler the following example may be what you want:
)

```d
import core.thread;

class MyThread : Thread
{
    this( void function() fn, size_t sz = 0 ) @safe pure nothrow @nogc
    {
        super(fn, sz);
    }

    this( void delegate() dg, size_t sz = 0 ) @safe pure nothrow @nogc
    {
        super(dg, sz);
    }

    override void filterCaughtThrowable(ref Throwable t) @system nothrow
    {
        import core.stdc.stdio;
        import core.stdc.stdlib;

        if (auto e = cast(Error) t)
        {
            auto msg = e.message();
            fprintf(stderr, "Thread death due to error: %.*s\n", cast(int)msg.length, msg.ptr);
            fflush(stderr);
            abort();
        }

        super.filterCaughtThrowable(t);
    }
}
```
)


)

$(BUGSTITLE_TEXT_BODY Library changes,

$(LI $(LNAME2 variant_disabled_this,variant: Support big structs with `@disable this()`)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE phobos, changelog/variant_disabled_this.dd)
$(P
std.variant didn't compile when the payload was a struct that was bigger than
its internal buffer, had to be allocated on the heap, and had `@disable this()`.
The new handling now always skips the constructor and just copies the payload
directly to the allocated region.
)
)


)

$(BUGSTITLE_TEXT_BODY Dub changes,

$(LI $(LNAME2 dustmite-timeout,Added `--timeout` option to `dub dustmite` command.)
$(CHANGELOG_SOURCE_FILE dub, changelog/dustmite-timeout.dd)
$(P
Adds a timeout (in seconds) for each oracle invocation, preventing
the dustmite process from hanging indefinitely when the test command
does not terminate. Requires the `timeout` command to be available
(coreutils on Linux/macOS).
)
)


)

